---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import GatewayCard from '@/components/ia/GatewayCard.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---

<Layout title="Semantic modelling" description="Learn what ontologies are, why SPDTF uses them, and how the SPDTF ontology is modelled, reviewed and implemented.">
  <h1>Semantic modelling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Better property data starts with shared understanding. We bring practitioners'
    language, evidence and experience into a connected model that people can read,
    challenge and improve. You do not need to know ontology engineering to take part.
  </p>
  <p>
    A conveyancer, lender and surveyor can describe the same property from different
    perspectives. Our approach preserves the distinctions they need and makes the
    agreements between them explicit. The website is where that model becomes
    visible: definitions, examples, diagrams, controlled choices and questions for review.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose">Choose your route</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/why-ontologies" eyebrow="For everyone" title="Understand ontologies" description="What shared meaning makes possible, why property data needs it, and how you can contribute your professional knowledge without learning technical notation." />
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-method" eyebrow="For modellers and implementers" title="How we model SPDTF" description="Our context-first manifesto and normative modelling rules: identity, roles, phases, SKOS, SHACL, context maps, evidence and connected semantic packages." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="start-here">Start with the question that brought you here</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/benefits" title="What difference will this make?" description="Understand how clearer meaning can improve reuse, reveal mismatches and make evidence easier to follow." />
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/taking-part" title="How can I help?" description="Find a familiar topic, challenge a definition and explain an exception through the website and working groups." />
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/principles" title="What is different about our approach?" description="Start with local meaning and accountable communities, then connect their models through explicit agreements." />
    <GatewayCard href="/semantic-modelling/coverage" title="What exactly are we modelling?" description="The eight selected ODR-0071 concerns, their relationship to the six outputs, and what is deliberately outside scope." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="one-agreement">Two routes, one agreement</h2>
  <p>
    Participants and implementers see different representations, but neither route owns
    a separate definition of the standard. Business review establishes intended meaning;
    technical artefacts encode and test that reviewed candidate meaning.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The teaching and implementation routes meet at one governed candidate. Business review remains the authority for meaning; technical artefacts make that meaning testable and reusable.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Two routes into one governed semantic candidate
  accDescr: Nontechnical learning leads to business review while technical documentation leads to encoded artefacts and checks; both meet at one governed candidate.
  Learn["Understand ontologies"]:::user
  Review["Review definitions and examples"]:::user
  Method["Follow the modelling method"]:::process
  Encode["Encode and test artefacts"]:::data
  Candidate["One versioned candidate"]:::service
  Decision["Recorded human disposition"]:::security
  Projection["Tested delivery projections"]:::external
  Learn --> Review
  Method --> Encode
  Review --> Candidate
  Encode --> Candidate
  Candidate --> Decision
  Decision --> Projection
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="documented">What this section documents</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Scope and authority of the semantic-modelling documentation</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">What you will find</th><th scope="col">Authority boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Business meaning</th><td>Definitions, examples, identities, relationships and questions that practitioners can review.</td><td>Relevant working groups judge domain meaning.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Modelling method</th><td>The selected ODR-0071 categories and supporting rules, evidence intake, competency questions and review.</td><td>The relevant source ODRs are normative; ADR-0063 and ADR-0067 apply the scoped method. Wider lifecycle proposals in ADR-0065/0068 remain separate.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Technical implementation</th><td>RDF/RDFS/OWL, SKOS, SHACL, SPARQL, metadata and generated projections.</td><td>Each feature needs a version, bounded profile and test receipt.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Standards and methods</th><td>Required modelling discipline, selected vocabulary profiles and what the Property Pack candidate actually implements.</td><td>A required design is distinct from demonstrated implementation or whole-ontology import.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Validation</th><td>Parsing, constraints, competency queries, semantic review and governance promotion.</td><td>Automated success does not approve business meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="terms">A one-minute vocabulary</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Ontology</dt><dd>A shared, explicit model of the things that matter, their meanings, identities and relationships.</dd>
    <dt>Resource</dt><dd>Anything identified in the graph: for example a property, title, address, organisation, role or governed concept.</dd>
    <dt>Class</dt><dd>A category used to describe what kind of thing a resource is.</dd>
    <dt>Property</dt><dd>A named relationship to another resource or a named value such as a date or text.</dd>
    <dt>Concept scheme</dt><dd>A governed set of values or concepts expressed with SKOS.</dd>
    <dt>Shape</dt><dd>A SHACL description of what a data graph is expected to contain for a particular use.</dd>
    <dt>Projection</dt><dd>A generated schema, form, API, page or document for a particular consumer.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="example">The Property Pack is a worked component, not the whole ontology</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack ontology</a> is the first accelerated
    SPDTF component. Its current machine-proposed model demonstrates the technical
    patterns described here and is awaiting a Technical Working Group determination.
    It does not settle the wider ontology method by itself, and successful validation
    does not turn it into an approved standard.
  </p>
  <div class="callout callout--key">
    <p class="callout__label">Read status literally</p>
    <p>“Used in Property Pack 0.1,” “accepted SPDTF target,” “candidate,” “method lens” and “adopted SPDTF release” are different statements. This section keeps them separate throughout.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision guide</a> connects this
    section to its normative ODRs, OPDA ADRs and useful legacy PDTF explanations.
    For a practical starting point, <a href="/semantic-modelling/questions">read the
    common questions</a> or <a href="/join">join a working group</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
